Not that I-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] not that I believed in it. I still don't believe. Because, well, my generation is a cursed generation. We are always hoping. We never give up looking.

Since you don't see the body put into the ground, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] you think maybe you hear one out of a million that someone met after so many years. And we cannot bring it to ourself to understand that if they didn't come till now, no one will come. But we're searching. We're gathering. We're getting together maybe.

So I was sent from that camp in Sosnowiec. I was sent to a woman's forced labor camp in Neusalz. That was Germany, not far from Breslau.

INT: How would you spell that camp name?

FW: Neusalz?

INT: Mhm.

FW: N-E-U-- N-E-U-S-A-L-Z. Neusalz.

INT: And the place that you were sent from before that?

FW: That was Sosnowiec. S-O-S-- and that was a city that Jews lived there, like in Chrzanów. But there, they had, like, a camp transfer. In other words, everybody came there, and from there, they transferred either to work or to Auschwitz. So this was in town of Sosnowiec, S-O-S-N-O-W-I-E-C.

INT: And when you got to Neusalz?

FW: To Neusalz, there were already some girls there from previous years. It was a thousand-- a camp for thousand women. And everybody worked in a different-- we-- they-- what we-- they made, they made yarn for [NON-ENGLISH]. [NON-ENGLISH] was a very famous sewing yarn.

So from the field [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] where they cut down the-- for the yarn, where they cut down the linen, brought to a factory, till they manufactured yarn on spools. I worked in the part of the factory where I worked already from big logs of yarn. It came [INAUDIBLE] by the machine with spool-- on spools.

Then we unloaded this part of spools. It came, you know, and they would empty spools. We loaded up with spools, empty ones, and that's where I worked. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It was tough for me.

I-- I was very hungry in that camp. I was hungry already in Sosnowiec, when I was-- I was a big-- I was a big girl, and I was a big eater. And the bigger of a eater you are, the more you suffer.

Because-- and then you-- if you don't get the food, you become weak. You lose weight. Because there is some people that whatever they have, for a while, they have enough. They're small eaters.

And in that camp, I really had nobody. There were some girls yet that had some parents in towns like-- and they were-- they were allowed to receive packages, so they used to get them. I had nobody anymore. And so I had to eat the rations that they gave me. And was not enough for me. I was very hungry.

As a matter of fact, where we lived, in the-- in the barrack, there was-- on a Sunday, it was a free day-- there was very beautiful green grass growing. So there was one girl from our hometown that used to cut down with a knife the grass, wash it, and put it-- because we used to get the four slices of bread, but they were as thin, almost, as a nail.

So she used to put the grass between two slices of that, as if it were like a cracker, that were like a pumpernickel here that you buy in stores, you know, very thin sliced. And we used to ask her. I mean, what-- she said, look, I'm very hungry. I have to bite into something, and I have to chew something.

And if the cows don't die from it, I'm not die-- I am not going to die from it either. So later on, I-- I did that too because we worked hard and not enough food. Was a time where there was no salt anymore, so we only got, like, some leaves dipped in water, almost cool, with sand not washed out, without salt.

And from there, I was there till 1944. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I worked in the factory with one Ukrainian girl. Because on one side of the machine, I was working, and on the other side of the machine, the Ukrainian girl was working. And there she was not alone.

There was quite a lot Ukrainian girls, but they were free. They lived outside of our camp, also in, like, condominiums, or, you know, like college students live on a, you know, campus, like a campus. But they could go. They were free, where we had to be brought to work by three Gestapo women, to and from.

So our work was-- if I-- my shift was in the morning, from 8:00 to 2:00, we went back to the camp. They took us home. 4 o'clock, we had to go back to the factory to work overtime two more hours, from 4:00 to 6:00. If we-- I worked the second shift, from 2:00 to 10:00, then we went from 11:00 till 1:00 for-- on overtime.

So we worked like 10 hours a day. And it was quite hard to take off that full thing from the machine and pick it up. My shoulders were very sore. And-- but we were there, quite peaceful. That means I-- I was not afraid as I was later and before. Because somehow, it got to be like a way of life. And you get used to hunger also.

So you walk around like a dog that's always looking something, a hungry dog that's looking with sometimes to pick up something from the street. But at least the fear was not there. Till-- but the problem was with this camp that if you got sick, Gestapo came and took you back, back to Sosnowiec, and from there, they sent you to Auschwitz.

One day, they took us to take an x-ray. And there were two girls from my hometown that apparently, their x-ray did not show very well. For a week, they didn't go to work.

And then there came a car and took them to Sosnowiec. I found out after the war, that one jumped off-- out of the window, she-- hearing that she's going to Auschwitz. But none of them-- one died, and one was sent to Auschwitz, did not survive.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] in 19-- I gave-- the girl, the Russian girl that worked with me on the other side of the machine, she had to go home, because her mother died, and they got only paid every second week. She didn't have any money.

I had 40-- 40 marks, D-marks. That's German money. Because we were allowed, for every person that-- that was sent out from Chrzanów, you could take $20, 20 marks. So for my brother and I, I had 40 marks sewed in in one of the-- in-- I wore the striped-- but in my coat, I've got it sewed in in the cushions.

So I took 20 marks and gave-- and gave her. And she promised-- she went for the funeral. And two weeks later, she promised me that she's going to bring me from the city potato salad and coleslaw, which was 35 cents a pound at that time. But I said, fine.

And then she brought me once. And somehow, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] someone reported her, and the Gestapo came to the camp one day, took me away, and took another girl away, from a different town, from our camp, and took us to jail in the same town. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

In the jail-- in jail, we were confronted with the girl-- I was confronted with the girl that I gave the 20 marks. And she-- they brought her up. She was beaten up. She was so black. Her eyes were bloodshot, and I didn't know what they want of me. I didn't know that that's what they want me to confess to. And I tried not to confess to anything.

So the Gestapo man, he asked me different questions, and he held a-- a pencil in his hand, played from one hand to the other side, see that he's taking the pencil from one hand to the other, and he wants to beat me. And that's when I told him what I did, that I gave this girl that they brought up 20 marks. And that was it.

We sat for three days in jail there, and then we were brought to Breslau, Germany. This is-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] now it belongs to Poland, but at that-- they gave it to Poland, but now, at that time, it was Germany. And this was also a camp where everybody from there went to Auschwitz.

They brought from different jails, from different camps, all people that did something. They tried to run away, or they did something according to the Germans, whatever they did. That's where they brought us. They brought me.

INT: Why did they object to your giving her the 20 marks?

FW: Because was a black market. Why-- how could I give her 20 marks for-- I didn't-- I didn't want any more. I just wanted-- but and then it was forbidden to bring the Jew, to a-- to a Jew, to bring food. They objected to that too. So--

INT: What happened to you in that jail for those three days?

FW: For the three days?

INT: Mhm.

FW: It was-- no, just a second. In jail, we were more than 10 days. Because it was so nice in that jail, because it was only the two girls, Jewish girls, from our camp, I and the other girl, that were sitting there. Otherwise, it was all Germans. And the Germans did get packages from home, so they didn't eat the food that the jail provided.

So we-- at the end of the evening, when the warden got through distributing the food, he always brought us a jug with food that was leftover, because the others, the German women, didn't eat it. And then we had, every day, a half an hour, we did a walk, like, you know, inside the-- inside the jail. We walked. That was our daily walk.

Every-- not only us. That was all the women that the-- in two, the one that you were sitting with in-- in jail. And we were walking. That was a daily walk. That's it. Right? So that--

INT: That was 10 days?

FW: I think he wants us to [INAUDIBLE]? [LAUGHS]

INT: I just want to finish this part.

FW: Yes.

INT: So you stayed there for 10 days?

FW: We stayed there for 10 days. And I-- by walking, it was a big walk. I mean, the place where we were walking, outside, inside of the jail, was surrounded by a tall, red wall made out of bricks, red bricks. And walking one day, I noticed with a-- written on the brick that one girl that was taken away from our camp, Neusalz, and she wrote in Polish that she was there, and after 14 days went to Auschwitz.INT: And I'm interviewing Faye Waldman, in Lincolnwood, Illinois, and this is tape three.

You were telling us about you were in jail, and you saw the name of a girl you knew on the wall.

FW: Yes.

INT: And--

FW: That girl was taken away from our camp-- from the camp the I was taken away also. Because the boss in the factory where she worked dropped over the fence on a Saturday, a cucumber from his garden [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] to her, and a carrot. And the lageralteste-- that means the chief of the camp-- we had a woman-- she saw it. And she reported it to the Gestapo.

And they came after her, and she disappeared. But we didn't know where she went. And that was the crime that she did, picked up the cucumber and a carrot that her own boss gave her.

The man was also-- the German boss was taken also away. We don't know what happened to him.

INT: And on the wall, it said that she was going to Auschwitz?

FW: That she knew, yes. She said that she went to Auschwitz. After 14 days being there, I went to Auschwitz. And coming from the town where we are, everybody knew Auschwitz. This was already '43-- Auschwitz was '41, '42.

So this-- so from then on, when we saw this, the girl that I was together with, we knew where we going to go. There was no day and no night.

INT: And how did you react to that?

FW: We-- how we reacted? There was no-- as I said, we had no peace, no day and no night. We couldn't sleep, we couldn't eat. The food that he brought us, we didn't even touch it, that the warden brought us. We knew we are going to Auschwitz. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

What happened that after 10 days, two German policemen came to us, took us out of jail, and took us through the town to go to a doctor to have-- to be checked if we are healthy enough to go to Auschwitz. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] On the way, they said to each other, the two policemen, you take one, and I take the other one, and let's just walk so people will not be watching that we are-- you know, that we are just watched by-- by the two of them.

So walking, he said to me, such a beautiful Jewish child. Why are you so sad? And I said, I'm sad because I know where I'm going. And I know what's waiting for me.

And he looks at me, and he said, how do you know where you're going? I said, I know that I'm going to Auschwitz. Said, where are you from? And I said, I'm from Chrzanów.

And he showed a picture, on the way to the doctor, he showed me a picture. He was one of the police that was called out from Berlin, the-- the-- the time where they send away-- when they made Chrzanów free of Jews. He was helping the local police.

And after Chrzanów was freed, there was no more Jews, they made a picture with the police of Chrzanów in the market place. And he showed me the market place. And he told me, don't be afraid, because Auschwitz, there is some people that work yet-- as long as they're strong to work.

So don't get sick here. Maybe you be able to work. And he told me that, I can give you something to go by. He said, you should dig with your nose in the ground, but don't ever say that you can't do it. You may survive.

I still didn't believe him, because I never knew. All we knew is that Auschwitz is a chimney and a gas chamber. We didn't know how many. We knew that no one came out alive, as long as we are-- we were home. So how did I know that there is still a possibility for some people to go to a camp and to work?

And-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and also, we were very sad because life in jail was very peaceful-- nothing to be afraid of. All we did, all day long, is we separated the stamps for-- stamps from the feathers. Apparently they made something for pillowcases-- or what-- filled pillowcases with it.

And we took a bath every day. We changed our clothes every day. And this came to end. On the 10th day, came a man, Gestapo, in civil-- in civilian clothes.

And he told us to sign some papers, because without our signature, [LAUGHS] they wouldn't send us. I asked him, without my signature, you wouldn't send me to Auschwitz? He said, he's going to be the one who is going to bring us to the station, and he's going to have some food for us.

And that was it. A day later, he came for us-- my girlfriend and I. Was taking to the-- taken to a station by a small car, covered up. And we came to the train that was standing to Auschwitz.

When we got in there, the man left. But what we saw was a train full of people. All of them were from camps. There were some men that must of run away-- they were chained, with chains, to their seats, their feet in their seat. And there were Russian girls, there were Ukrainian girls. And there was a-- the girl and I.

And we went to Auschwitz. And can you imagine that I passed by my hometown, coming from Breslau, Germany, to go to Auschwitz. Because that was the route. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

We got there, to Auschwitz-- it was a Saturday night, Saturday afternoon. We were brought to a barrack where they-- you take bath. Just all-- the barrack is like a-- you take-- they take everybody once in a blue moon to take a bath. There is hundreds of showers that you take a shower-- that was like a shower house.

Since Saturday is very quiet on the camp-- some day, there is no kommandos, nobody goes to-- nobody went to work. We stayed there all night long in that barrack. And Monday morning, they took-- Monday morning, they stripped us off our clothes, they put up tables, told us to make a line, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] put the tattoo on our arms, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] and came out with striped clothes, throwing it to about maybe 300 women, just throwing it.

If you were lucky, that you were small, and you got a small dress, you were lucky. I was tall, and I got a small dress. Someone very small could have gotten a dress that went up to the-- the-- to the feet down, all the way down. And that was to be worn for us till about four weeks later, when we were brought again to take a shower.

While standing there naked, men were coming that work there, plumbers-- all-- they-- they were all inmates. They were all [INAUDIBLE].

And we were standing there naked. The majority of the women who had their head cut-- her hair cut like a man. And I, because I don't know what kind of a-- I did never understood that, because I came from a jail, when they took my clothes away, they put it in a special sack and wrote down my name.

And they only made for me here, as wide as a clippers is here, in my-- they didn't cut my hair like a man's. They didn't shave it all the way. They just made right here a shave in the middle, like that.

And I was sent to a-- A lager. A lager was a lager that was a quarantine lager. That means that from there, you were six weeks there. And after six weeks, if you didn't get sick, didn't get diphtheria, didn't get the diarrhea, didn't get typhus, that's when they send you to a B lager, which was the working lager. This was lager A.

I remain-- I remained there. They never sent me any place else. Circumstances were awful.

We slept, eight women-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] on a-- it wasn't a bed, it was just build up, eight on top, and eight on the bottom. We got up in the morning, 6 o'clock, were chased out, outside, stayed till they took-- stayed still till they took the count.

If everything was OK, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they brought out on wheels black coffee. And the minute it got light outside, the-- the kapos came, whips to beat us, and to take us away to work. I didn't know what we were going to do.

But when they took us, there was a pile of stones then laying on one side, heavy stones. So they told us to bring the stones to the other side. That's what we did. If you were too slow, you got beaten.

At 12 o'clock, they brought us back to our-- two our barrack outside. And the people that worked inside in the barrack brought out from the main kitchen, again, a big kettle, with hot soup, whatever was in there. And they counted five women-- they gave-- they gave five spoons, ladles, into one dish, which was piled up next to the food kettle.

And hot-- no spoon, no nothing, we had to go on the side-- and just five women, five mouths, just sip from-- from that one thing. It was hot. We couldn't eat. 10 minutes later, the kapo came back, to stay in line, to take us back to work. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

This-- those stones that we carried from this side to the other side, now we had to bring them back to the side where we found them in the morning. That was our job, just to make you feel not human. Degrading-- you shouldn't have time-- not-- even the food you don't eat, because you can't eat. How can you eat standing hot, and five women around one plate? [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

[SIGHS]

Then, 5 o'clock, you go back to the same thing-- that you stay in line, they count, someone is missing. I was on the block together with women from Greece, with French women, Jewish women, and with some Ukrainians. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Those Greek women, they didn't speak any English. They speak-- spoke only Hebrew and Greek. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] They were beaten. They were not understood. They got sick because-- when it's cold, they-- in Greece, they're not used to cold weather.

So, and from the food, whatever-- if they ever ate anything, they got diarrhea. So one woman, we had one block latrine-- latrine, you know, where-- just holes, and you sat down. She was sick, and she had to go, and she didn't make it back for the count.

So they brought her back-- they found her after an hour, we were standing, because they didn't let you go till they-- the count was right. I mean, if one was missing, that-- they had to look for her.

And they looked, they found her, they brought her. We had a hole dug in the middle of-- of the place where we stayed, where we were. And there was little stones, pointed stones, little like-- you see around the trees, people by those white stones, very sharp.

The kapo, after she called off us we could go into the room, she gave her two big bricks, she told her to kneel down into that hole, with the-- with the stones and keep your-- her hands up with the bricks. The next morning when we were called out, she was dead. We don't know how long she was dead, but that was her punishment for being sick, and-- and staying in the latrine.

Besides that, when we go into the-- when we go into the-- our barrack, people have to-- people were sick-- people got sick from-- they had diarrhea. So there was a wagon that stood in the middle as you got out, and people went out. Because you were not-- it was [NON-ENGLISH]. We were not supposed to leave our block.

Because the chimneys-- the sky was the red, the chimneys were burning full blast. And the smell of-- of bones and hair was so thick, that your-- you couldn't-- your eyes were watering. So you could only go if you had to go-- to make. It was-- they-- it was running-- I mean, these women, things were running from them. They had nothing.

In other words, it was diarrhea so-- and so one girl, standing in line, made. So the same thing happened to her, what happened to that woman that they found in there. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

I was in Auschwitz [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] till January 18, 1945.

[PAUSES FOR 12 SECONDS]

OK.

INT: And then what happened?

FW: January 18th-- January 17th, in the evening, we were taking-- taken out to main-- to-- to Auschwitz. I was in Birkenau, I was not in Auschwitz. This is all Birkenau where I was sending. Were taken to a concert.

INT: What kind of a concert?

FW: That was concert where, now, inmates, men and women, played piano and instruments. And they entertained the Germans. So to fill in the room, they took us for a concert.

Standing-- going back to the camp, we marched, of course, every 10, 12 women had a Gestapo watching with the--

When we went back to the camps, to the camp, we saw people standing on the side with young children, with-- and speaking to each other. And one of them said in Jewish, that-- we were made to think-- we had on the-- while marching back from the concert, they told us what to sing, and we had to sing.

So as we entered the gate to go back to our camp, these women-- that stood there in the dark-- it was dark because the Russians were already bombing all around. And they said, I heard one woman saying, look, they're singing here. That can't be so bad. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

The next morning, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] these women came from ghetto Plaszów-- that was when they liquidated the ghetto Plaszów. These women were brought, with the children, to Auschwitz.

They had empty barracks, before-- few months ago, they took all the Gypsies that stayed with their families, that they brought them there, they burned one night. So these barracks were empty. They put these women and the children together.

And we taught those who were there a long-- longer time, we knew that that's something not kosher. Because if they let-- if they didn't segregate them, and they didn't-- they let the children be with their mothers, there must be something going on.

Sure enough, the next day, the 18th of January, there was an announcement. Everybody stood in front of his barracks. There was a row of barracks, and we were told that we are marching out of Auschwitz. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

Well, there was on the same camp where I was-- the Gypsy camp they called it, after they got rid of all the Gypsies-- there was also blocks, they called it revier for sick people. So they also went there to let the sick people know, that in case they want-- whoever is able, an able body, should stay in line and walk-- march out from Auschwitz.

Well, these people were too sick. And I was picked, with another three girls, to stay in the bread magazine to give some bread to the sick people, to leave some bread. So we left not on the morning of the 18th, I left already in the evening with other people, yet.

And as we left the main gate, we saw bundles of papers in front of every gate, burning already. They were burning already the documents, and all the what they had. And we saw, on both sides of the road, dead people laying from the morning, that they shot.

The whole road was paved with-- with bodies. Anyone who wanted to sit down to release himself, they didn't let him get up anymore. They shot. And then, a-- not a car, but a truck came, and picked the dead bodies, threw them on top, and--

We went like this [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] for the whole night, we marched. Because from then on, we could only march that-- march at night. Because during the day, bombs were falling left and right.

INT: So where were you?

FW: The Russians were-- during the day, we sat in the fields. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And during the night, we marched, so we couldn't be noticed. We saw in the back of us, the same night already, red sky. It looks like-- I think two days, or maybe a day after they took us from Auschwitz, the Russians came in. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

They didn't do anything to the dead ones-- they said they're going to gas them, they're going to-- the sick ones remained, whoever lived to see it. And we were marching like this. I was marching till the 8th of May.

INT: So how long was that all together?

FW: From January the 18th, till-- so it's-- so it's half-- from-- from January, February, March, April, May-- four-- four months and few weeks.

INT: And those who were left behind at Auschwitz were liberated on the second day?

FW: Yes. We were taken-- first, we were taken on the-- we were loaded on a big station. I didn't even know what station it was. We walked so far, and then they load on open wagons, like they transport cows, on open, open wagons.

And we were-- we went a mile or two, and we stopped because they were bombing. Again, we didn't know what the destination was. We just saw, because the wagons were open, when they left us there, the Germans ran into bunkers to hide. And they left us there.

And we saw like the planes were coming up, zoom! And at one point, a bomb fell about-- I-- I am-- I can't estimate, but very close to us. And it picked up our train, the whole train, just like picked up and went back from the impact of the bomb.

And when the SS came back to open the wagons, to see what happened to us, so they looked at us and they said-- showed us where the bomb fell. He said, you must have a good God-- they said it in German, [LAUGHS] that we lived through it, that nothing happened to us.

Anyways, they brought us to-- to Leipzig, which is a German town. And we were supposed-- there was a factory of ammunition, we were supposed to work there. We worked-- by the time we got there, by the time we got a decent sack to lay on, three days later, we were taken from there.

Because the-- the Russians were coming. And we were surrounded-- where we-- in Germany, where we were, Dresden, Leipzig, that was 80 kilometers in a circle from the Russians to the-- to the Allied-- Allied. So they wouldn't let us go.

So they-- we saw, we recognized places where we marched. We were here two days ago. And then you-- you recognize the same place because they didn't want to surround, and they didn't want to-- they didn't-- I mean, they didn't want to give up us. And that's how we were marching.

And as we were marching more and more, the more dead ones was on the march. At one time, we were bombed. The front of our marchers was bombed-- there were some 35 dead people from the bombs.

And by May, what we-- what we did, what we did is there was snow yet. There were little-- little towns, little villages. There was little snow on-- on the road. We marched only at night. And if you had something, a little can from something, you picked up snow. And that's what we ate.

One time, we got to a little town where they had a-- a magazine with rice, sacks, hundreds and hundreds sacks of rice. And we stayed in line, and we got two tablespoons of raw rice, to eat that.

INT: Fay, a magazine is a kind of a store?

FW: Yeah, a-- a magazine is a warehouse. Yeah, magazine, yeah, that's-- yeah, it's a warehouse.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we got the 6th or the-- no, maybe 3rd of-- of May, we got, in Germany, they took us to River [? Elbe-- ?] E-L-B-A. This is a river that divided the Russians from the Americans. One side-- that was the agreement, one side the Americans were supposed to take, and the other side, the Russians were supposed to take.

So what happened was, we were taken across the other-- to the American side with boats, little boats, like ferries. We stayed in the woods for a few hours. They've told us to rest.

At that time, we had to lay down on the bare-- and the ice was dripping already from the trees, because it was warm. And we were brought in, into a big farm, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] into-- in that-- maybe a few kilometers from the River [? Elbe ?] on the other side. That-- oh.INT: I'm interviewing Fay Waldman in Lincolnwood, Illinois. This is tape number four.

You were telling us how you crossed onto the other side of the Elbe, which should be the American side?

FW: Yes.

INT: And--

FW: And we were brought to a little village and-- which had a very big farmhouse. And the-- the farm-- the owners ran away apparently. No one was there. But the-- the man, the main man that worked for-- on the farmhouse was there. He let us in.

What our, the Gestapo that we came with, did, they were 500 Russian girls they put in one stable. They had huge stables. And 500, we were Jewish girls. So we were separated. The Russian girls were in one stable. We were in the other stable. And it was warm.

There was straw. There was. And we-- and the-- the Gestapo told us that we-- that's where we going to rest. And maybe we'll stay here for a while. And he locked-- he locked up the stables on the outside for the night. And he went-- he had a-- not apartment-- he had a room in the same village, across of the stables by a baker that used to-- that used to have a bakery in there.

But at night we heard a lot of plane action. I mean there was bombs falling. And so he came the next morning. He opened both doors of the stables. And he said we have to get out. We cannot stay here any longer.

People were very sick. People rested one day after-- and warm. They got sick. And we said-- I came out and with another girl, and we said we are not going go anyplace. We can't. We're going to die walking. So leave us here. Whatever will happen will happens.

He said, if we don't want to go, we have to-- he has to close the doors on the outside. And he's going to put some gas around, and he's going to burn-- put fire into the stables.

Well, the Russians went. Some of our Jewish women went with him, but we didn't. Some of them did not want to go anymore. And that was it. Whatever will be will be.

They left. A few minutes after, everybody smelled already that we thought we are on fire. He did not put any fire, just the fear of it. And if you're afraid you smell it already. And it's close to you. It's getting hot.

But what happened, within the next hour the innkeeper, the man that took care of-- of the whole farm while the owners were not there, came in. And he said if we'll-- no one is here. The Germans are not-- are gone. It's like a no-man's land here. If you send out two girls to the kitchen-- they used to have a tremendous kettle and a kitchen on the farm that they cooked potatoes for the chickens-- I don't know for whom-- we can cook potatoes in the skin-- with the skin.

And he has some milk that got sour because he did not have to deliver for the next two days to the main-- where they distributed the milk. And we gave-- that's what we did. We brought into the stable some milk, sour milk, and some potatoes. A lot of us got sick because hot potatoes and sour milk, we couldn't stomach it after not having food.

Anyways, what also happened the same evening, the Russians came across Elbe. They were not supposed to because this was already Allied territory. They came. They got into the stable. They took some girls with them. And they raped them. Then they brought them back.

So we saw that it is not safe to be there. So the delegation went to Dresden, to the man in command, which was the American command, to see something. Either he's going to send soldiers to watch us or to liberate us or to go someplace. We have no place to go. They told us that the roads are opened, that we can go whichever way. They don't have enough people to send people, American soldiers, to every little village or whatever.

So I picked myself up on the-- that was already 8, 10, maybe 18 or 19th of May, the 20th of May-- and went to the next railroad, where I could find a train. I found--

INT: Were you by yourself?

FW: Yes. I was by myself. And I didn't know who is alive. My first thoughts were that if there is anyone alive, remained from my family, we are going to meet in our hometown. So whoever is left will have a desire to get there. This is so.

So it took me two weeks to get home. Because there was no trains, you went few miles. You had to change. Then you were pushed away because there was transports of soldiers, Russian-- because Poland was under Russia already. And I came to my hometown. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I came to my hometown, and I stayed there with a man-- a-- a boy that also he was in Auschwitz. He used to come to my dad during the war to play cards with him, since there was nothing to do. He worked for the Sonderkommando in Auschwitz. He burned people. He took them out from the gas chamber and took them to the crematorium to be burned.

He was there. He already ran away from the-- from the march, from Auschwitz. We took him-- he took his apartment, threw away-- threw out the German, and found his sisters-- his sister's father-in-law. And they had two rooms, so I stayed there for a short time in the other room and found out that my uncle survived a camp and that he is staying in Weidenberg. This is a town in what was given to-- was taken away from the Germans, given to the Poles after the war.

I went there. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] on the way there, I've met someone that I knew from my hometown. It was my husband's brother. But at that time, I wasn't married. We lived on one street. So we knew each other. And he went to Weidenberg, where I was going, because his sister was getting married. So he was going to invite some people that lived there as guests. They were all in camps together. So--

And he told me that he lives in that little village there, 30 kilometers before Weidenberg. Ludwigsdorf is the name of it. And his brother-- that means my husband now-- is living with them. And they have three sisters that survived-- that five people, they have a house that they got from Germans, and they are living in the house.

And I went to see my uncle. He lived [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] under the roof, very, very high, where the-- the-- the-- it goes like this. You know, it's like an attic almost, dark. And he was sick. He lived with a German man that took care of him. I had no place to stay there. I had no place to be there.

That night I slept on the floor because with two men, where there was only one room. And there was nothing that I could do for my uncle. I wasn't-- I-- I didn't know what to do for him. The German was taking better care of him, taking, than I would because he knew already. And so I went back to the village where my husband, at that time, lived with his family.

And I remained there and married my husband. This was already June 1945, or maybe July. And I married him the following year, in March 1946. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

We got married. We stayed. This was under Polish occupation, Poland-- Russians, Polish yet, and there was no way for us to stay there any longer. There was no means of making a living. There was nothing. So we thought we are going to smuggle ourselves through a border and to come somewhere to the zone, to the US zone in-- in Germany. There was a US zone, and there was the Russians had this-- we wanted to go to the Allied, you know, to the US zone.

And we [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] paid someone who knew the way to goes. And we came to-- to Germany. We came to Germany. And we were taken by a truck, apparently hired American, hired by someone. We paid money to bring us to a camp, where it was also all the people that wanted to be in the American zone, stayed there. And we got our rations there. And it was not far from Austria.

INT: How did you have money at that point to pay?

FW: How did we have money at that--

INT: Yeah.

FW: --point to pay? I sold my husband's-- my father's house where we lived for $850 when I went back home. As long as I live there in Ludwigsdorf, I sold the house to a Pole. And that's how we had money. We bought-- we bought some dollars for that Polish money, because we knew with the Polish money we are not going to get anywhere. And that's how we paid for the men to bring us over to the-- [DRINKS]

And then we landed in Austria, Vienna. They called it the Rothschild Hotel, but it was not a hotel. It was a transit camp for the people that wanted to go to America. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So we, just like now, they bring the Jews from Russia. They bring them to Vienna or to Italy. And that's-- and from there, I did have an uncle here that was in contact with me yet when I came to-- when I came back after the war.

He wrote. There was also a representative in my home-- in my Chrzanów. There was after the war made a little committee that represented the Jews that lived yet-- came back and lived yet in Chrzanów after the war-- after the war. Some of them did yet. So my uncle wrote a little note to that committee with a question, if they know what happened to his family. So they wrote him back.

So they gave me that card. When I came to sell my father's house, they gave me that card. And that's how I got in touch with my uncle.

INT: Which uncle was it?

FW: That was the one that I have the picture. Oh, we didn't do the pictures yet. Huh?

INT: Yeah. Whose brother was he?

FW: That was my father's brother.

INT: And what was his name?

FW: [? Leo. ?] He came here in 1937 from Germany. He ran away just in time. And he was the one that brought me here to America.

But I did have a baby in Germany. My daughter was born in 1947. We-- we-- oh, we came to Germany, to Bavaria.

INT: After Vienna?

FW: After Vienna. We came to Germany, to Bavaria. And we settled down in a town, little town, Münchberg-- M-U-N-C-H-B-E-R-G. And there we've got the room, which was taken away from a German that had a whole apartment, with a separate entry. And that's where I lived in Germany till my daughter was born, 1947, June the 19th. I was actually-- I was pregnant already after we-- when we left Poland.

This is it. [INAUDIBLE]. So anyways, he does-- she was born there. We still lived in that one room. But my uncle sent me an affidavit. I got in touch, contact with him. And he sent me an affidavit to come to-- to Chicago.

Besides this, also there was a change in-- there was a change in-- in-- in-- in the procedure because Truman passed a law that 10,000 DPs can come to America without even an affidavit, because it was very hard to get an affidavit for just anyone. They had to show that they have enough money, that you're not going to fall as a burden to the government. And so it-- it required a lot.

So this way, without any papers, without any-- they-- they let in 10,000, not necessarily Jews, 10,000 DPs, which is this abbreviation for displaced person.

INT: So you came as part of that group?

FW: I came as part of that group, to New York.

INT: What was the date?

FW: The date was August 23, 1949.

INT: And what has been-- what have been the highlights since you've been here in America?

FW: What are the highlights? We-- we worked hard. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We went to school. After 9 and 1/2 years I had another baby. My son was born. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

I feel that my past will never be forgotten,

[BACKGROUND NOISE]

